{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-120758" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=3{t}^{2}-8t+4,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  The positive direction is left to right. If the particle is stationary when $t=2\\,\\text{s},$ determine the time intervals in which the particle moves to the right." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-120758" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}-8t+4)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}+4t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(2)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(2)^{3}-4(2)^{2}+4(2)+C \\\\ 0 & =8-16+8+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}+4t.$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the particle is moving to the *right*, we must solve the inequality $v>0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}+4t=t(t-2)^{2}>0.\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t)$, we get the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/assets/9254ea32fc55b2a703e52f55f4292d9708eef63ae147a9f1d2eac40b85eff319.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $v>0$ whenever $0<t<2$ or $t>2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-120758" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f8cd67100a32b5ea130c8350bd12e18097682079c03ce198bae0e62d793d5d80"} {"question" "q-120758" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-120758" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0<t<2"] ["math" "2<t<3"] ["math" "t>1"] ["math" "t>2"] ["text" "$0<t<2$ and $t>2$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-120758" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$0<t<2$ and $t>2$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-81769" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=1-2t$, where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. The positive direction is left to right. If the particle has velocity $v=-6\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=4\\,\\text{s}$, determine the time intervals in which the particle moves to the right." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-81769" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (1-2t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t-{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(4)=-6.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6 & =(4)-(4)^{2}+C \\\\ -6 & =4-16+C \\\\ -6 & =-12+C \\\\ C & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=-{t}^{2}+t+6.$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the particle is moving to the *right*, we must solve the inequality $v>0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n-{t}^{2}+t+6=-({t}^{2}-t-6)=-(t+2)(t-3)>0.\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t),$ we get the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/assets/74db16dc00f5d08637cef02960409b7fd15c4bd3c19f577fa5a1f30ac8c402d3.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $v>0$ whenever $0<t<3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-81769" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f8cd67100a32b5ea130c8350bd12e18097682079c03ce198bae0e62d793d5d80"} {"question" "q-81769" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-81769" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0<t<3"] ["math" "2<t<4"] ["math" "3<t<6"] ["math" "t>3"] ["math" "t>6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-81769" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0<t<3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-120755" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ $a(t)=2t-5,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. The positive direction is left to right. If the particle has velocity $v=10\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=6\\,\\text{s},$ determine the time intervals in which the particle moves to the left." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-120755" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2t-5)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}-5t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(6)=10.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =(6)^{2}-5(6)+C \\\\ 10 & =6+C \\\\ C & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{2}-5t+4.$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the particle is moving to the *left*, we must solve the inequality $v<0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}-5t+4=(t-1)(t-4)<0.\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t)$, we get the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/assets/ad76f817f7c6e4a254e8881c34270eb875e72d821531566cb141cb393dcead72.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $v<0$ whenever $1<t<4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-120755" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f8cd67100a32b5ea130c8350bd12e18097682079c03ce198bae0e62d793d5d80"} {"question" "q-120755" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-120755" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0<t<1"] ["math" "0<t<4"] ["math" "1<t<4"] ["math" "t>1"] ["math" "t>4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-120755" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1<t<4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-120757" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=3{t}^{2}-6t-4,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  The positive direction is left to right. If the particle is stationary when $t=4\\,\\text{s},$ determine the time intervals in which the particle moves to the left." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-120757" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}-6t-4)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-4t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(4)^{3}-3(4)^{2}-4(4)+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-4t.$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the particle is moving to the *left*, we must solve the inequality $v<0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-4t=t(t-4)(t+1)<0.\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t)$, we get the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/assets/002f547b882940d087d6ed9d228334ec163931cfea0665eafdfe8b6ec8d863da.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $v<0$ whenever $0<t<4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-120757" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f8cd67100a32b5ea130c8350bd12e18097682079c03ce198bae0e62d793d5d80"} {"question" "q-120757" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-120757" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0<t<3"] ["math" "0<t<4"] ["math" "1<t<4"] ["math" "t>0"] ["math" "t>4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-120757" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0<t<4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-354843" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A runner moves along a straight track relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=3{t}^{2}-2t-12,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. The positive direction is left to right. If the runner is stationary when $t=4\\,\\text{s},$ determine the time interval in which the runner moves to the left.\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$<t<$\n\n{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-354843" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}-2t-12)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-{t}^{2}-12t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(4)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(4)^{3}-(4)^{2}-12(4)+C \\\\ 0 & =64-16-48+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the runner at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{3}-{t}^{2}-12t.$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the runner is moving to the *left*, we must solve the inequality $v<0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}-12t=t(t-4)(t+3)<0.\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t)$, we get the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/assets/27b9a41b0ad5c3ff98f6d76e24f0dc74e863d8f63a4c033fc41b7f7f2ebd72ea.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $v<0$ whenever $0\\,<\\,t\\,<\\,4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-354843" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f8cd67100a32b5ea130c8350bd12e18097682079c03ce198bae0e62d793d5d80"} {"question" "q-354843" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-354843" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-354843" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-354843" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-120784" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=15-3t,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=14\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s},$ calculate the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}$ of the particle and determine the time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-120784" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 15-3t & =0 \\\\ t & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $t=5\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the second derivative test. For this, we find the second derivative of $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(15-3t) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}(5)<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=5\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (15-3t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =15t-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(1)=14.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15(1)-\\frac{3}{2}(1)^{2}+C & =14 \\\\ C & =\\frac{3}{2}-1\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{1}{2}+15t-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}.$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{\\text{max}}=v(5)=\\frac{1}{2}+15(5)-\\frac{3}{2}(5)^{2}=38\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-120784" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f8cd67100a32b5ea130c8350bd12e18097682079c03ce198bae0e62d793d5d80"} {"question" "q-120784" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-120784" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{v}_{\\text{max}}=14\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=1\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "{v}_{\\text{max}}=24.5\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=2\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "{v}_{\\text{max}}=37.5\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=5\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "{v}_{\\text{max}}=38\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=5\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "{v}_{\\text{max}}=75\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=5\\,\\text{s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-120784" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{v}_{\\text{max}}=38\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=5\\,\\text{s}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-49950" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=2-\\frac{2}{3}t,$ where $t⩾0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s},$ calculate the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}$ of the particle and determine the time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-49950" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 2-\\frac{2}{3}t & =0 \\\\ t & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $t=3\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the second derivative test. For this, we find the second derivative of $v(t)$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-\\frac{2}{3}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}(3)<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=3\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2-\\frac{2}{3}t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2t-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)-\\frac{1}{3}(0)^{2}+C & =4 \\\\ 0+0+C & =4\\,⟹\\,C=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2t-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{2}+4.$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{\\text{max}}=v(3)=2(3)-\\frac{1}{3}(3)^{2}+4=7\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-49950" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f8cd67100a32b5ea130c8350bd12e18097682079c03ce198bae0e62d793d5d80"} {"question" "q-49950" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-49950" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{v}_{\\text{max}}=13\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=2\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "{v}_{\\text{max}}=4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=3\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "{v}_{\\text{max}}=7\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=3\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "{v}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{20}{3}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=2\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "{v}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{29}{3}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=1\\,\\text{s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-49950" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{v}_{\\text{max}}=7\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=3\\,\\text{s}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-354998" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t),$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s},$ calculate the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}$ of the particle and determine the earliest time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity.\n\nThe maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ is reached first time at $t=$\n{{field-2}}$\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-354998" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$  \n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & =0 \\\\ 5t & =\\pi n \\\\ t & =\\frac{\\pi }{5}n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer.  \n\nTherefore, the extrema of the velocity function, for $t>0,$ are\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{5},\\frac{2\\pi }{5},\\frac{3\\pi }{5},…\n$$\n\nWe now test the extrema values, starting with the least, using the second derivative test. For this, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\  & =50\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $t=\\frac{\\pi }{5},$ the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{5}) & =50\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{5}) \\\\  & =50\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =-50<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{5})<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{5}.$ Therefore, the first time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity is $t=\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+C & =2 \\\\ -2+C & =2 \\\\ C & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nv(t)=4-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{\\text{max}} & =v(\\frac{\\pi }{5}) \\\\  & =4-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{5}) \\\\  & =4-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =6\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the maximum velocity  ${v}_{\\text{max}}=6\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ is reached first time at  $t=\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\,\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-354998" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f8cd67100a32b5ea130c8350bd12e18097682079c03ce198bae0e62d793d5d80"} {"question" "q-354998" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-354998" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-354998" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-354998" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi}{5}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-354997" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=3\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s},$ calculate the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}$ of the particle and determine the earliest time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity.\n\nThe maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ is reached first time at $t=$\n{{field-2}}$\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-354997" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$  \n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =0 \\\\ 4t & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n \\\\ t & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer.  \n\nTherefore, the extrema of the velocity function, for $t>0,$ are\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\frac{3\\pi }{8},\\frac{5\\pi }{8},…\n$$\n\nWe now test the extrema values, starting with the least, using the second derivative test. For this, we find the second derivative of $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)) \\\\  & =-48\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8},$ the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =-48\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =-48\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-48\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-48<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}.$  Therefore, the first time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity is $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=3.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+C & =3 \\\\ 0+C & =3\\,⟹\\,C=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nv(t)=3+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{\\text{max}} & =v(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =3+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =3+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =6\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the maximum velocity  ${v}_{\\text{max}}=6\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ is reached first time at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\,\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414