ext{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=2\\,\\text{s},$ calculate the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}$ of the particle and determine the time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-120817" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 16t-{t}^{3} & =0 \\\\ t(16-{t}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ t(4-t)(4+t) & =0 \\\\ t & =0,\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t>0$, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the second derivative test. For this, we find the second derivative of $v(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{″}(t) & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(16t-{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =16-3{t}^{2} \\\\ {v}^{″}(4) & =16-3(4)^{2} \\\\  & =-32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}(4)<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (16t-{t}^{3})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(2)=44.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8(2)^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}(2)^{4}+C & =44 \\\\ 32-4+C & =44\\,⟹\\,C=16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=16+8{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{4}.$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{\\text{max}}=v(4)=16+8(4)^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}(4)^{4}=80\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-120817" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f8cd67100a32b5ea130c8350bd12e18097682079c03ce198bae0e62d793d5d80"} {"question" "q-120817" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-120817" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{v}_{\\text{max}}=144\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=4\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "{v}_{\\text{max}}=22\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=1\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "{v}_{\\text{max}}=44\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=2\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "{v}_{\\text{max}}=64\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=4\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "{v}_{\\text{max}}=80\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=4\\,\\text{s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-120817" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{v}_{\\text{max}}=80\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=4\\,\\text{s}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-120847" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=\\sqrt{t}-t,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s},$ determine the moment in time when the particle is stationary." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-120847" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\sqrt{t}-t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(1)=\\frac{1}{6}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} & =\\frac{2}{3}(1)^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{6} & =\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{6} & =\\frac{1}{6}+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}.$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}=0 \\\\ 4{t}^{3/2}-3{t}^{2}=0 \\\\ {t}^{3/2}(4-3{t}^{1/2}) & =0 \\\\ t & =0,\\,\\frac{16}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t>0,$ the particle is stationary when $t=\\frac{16}{9}\\,\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-120847" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f8cd67100a32b5ea130c8350bd12e18097682079c03ce198bae0e62d793d5d80"} {"question" "q-120847" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-120847" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t=16\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "t=1\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "t=\\frac{16}{9}\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "t=\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "t=\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\text{s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-120847" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "t=\\frac{16}{9}\\,\\text{s}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-349452" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=4\\sqrt{t},$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=\\frac{7}{3}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s},$ determine the moment in time when the particle is stationary.\n\nThe particle is stationary at $t=$\n{{field-1}}$\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-349452" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (4\\sqrt{t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{t}^{3/2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(1)=\\frac{7}{3}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{3} & =\\frac{8}{3}(1)^{3/2}+C \\\\ \\frac{7}{3} & =\\frac{8}{3}+C \\\\ C & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{8}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{3} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{8}{3}{t}^{3/2} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 8{t}^{3/2} & =1 \\\\ {t}^{3/2} & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ t & ={(\\frac{1}{8})}^{2/3}=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary when $t=\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-349452" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f8cd67100a32b5ea130c8350bd12e18097682079c03ce198bae0e62d793d5d80"} {"question" "q-349452" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-349452" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-84233" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=1\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=9\\,\\text{s},$ determine the moment in time when the particle is stationary." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-84233" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(9)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\sqrt{9}+C \\\\ 1 & =3+C \\\\ C & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\sqrt{t}-2.$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{t}-2 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{t} & =2 \\\\ t & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary when $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-84233" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f8cd67100a32b5ea130c8350bd12e18097682079c03ce198bae0e62d793d5d80"} {"question" "q-84233" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-84233" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t=1\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "t=2\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "t=3\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "t=4\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "t=6\\,\\text{s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-84233" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "t=4\\,\\text{s}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-355266" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=\\frac{2}{t},$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t={e}^{3}\\,\\text{s}$, determine the moment in time when the particle is stationary. Do not round your answer.\n\nThe particle is stationary at $t=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-355266" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v({e}^{3})=4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3})+C \\\\ 4 & =6+C \\\\ C & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)-2.$ \n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)-2 & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(t) & =1 \\\\ t & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary when $t=e\\,\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-355266" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f8cd67100a32b5ea130c8350bd12e18097682079c03ce198bae0e62d793d5d80"} {"question" "q-355266" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-355266" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "e"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-120843" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=3{t}^{2}-6t-4,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=30\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=5\\,\\text{s},$ determine the moment in time when the particle is stationary." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-120843" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}-6t-4)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-4t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(5)=30.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30 & =(5)^{3}-3(5)^{2}-4(5)+C \\\\ 30 & =125-75-20+C \\\\ 30 & =30+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-4t.$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-4t & =0 \\\\ t({t}^{2}-3t-4) & =0 \\\\ t(t-4)(t+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $t=-1,0,4.$\n\nSince $t>0,$ we discard solutions $t=-1,0.$ \nTherefore, the particle is stationary when $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-120843" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f8cd67100a32b5ea130c8350bd12e18097682079c03ce198bae0e62d793d5d80"} {"question" "q-120843" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-120843" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t=0\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "t=1\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "t=2\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "t=4\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "t=5\\,\\text{s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "q-120843" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "t=4\\,\\text{s}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/state.json" "file_sha256" "269900e70d5414d13a47e98c1b3a6c3c5ab6155653a4308d8e395c75bc9180ad"} {"question" "e-564" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s^2,$ given by the function $a(t) = 3t^2+2t-6,$ where $t > 0$ is the time in seconds. The positive direction is left to right. If the particle is stationary when $t=0\\,\\textrm s,$ determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving to the right." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/example-564.json" "file_sha256" "8754342bf61ad02504e1bb37be04889e9c2fb389a23b0ddd4520bcedf3bd1aee"} {"question" "e-564" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}+2t-6)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}+{t}^{2}-6t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the particle is stationary at the time $t=0.$ This means $v(0) = 0,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(0)^{3}+(0)^{2}-6(0)+C \\\\ 0 & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore the velocity of the particle is\n\n$$\nv(t) = t^3+t^2-6t.\n$$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the particle is moving to the *right*, we must solve the inequality $v > 0.$ This gives\n\n$$\nt^3+t^2-6t = t(t+3)(t-2) > 0.\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t),$ we get the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/assets/03b5ff6282d59038cbb355c0c3f017b544f286228ba4c3a82329897b4b43674c.png)\n\nThe particle moves to the right when $v(t)$ is positive. So, from the graph, we see that the particle moves to the right when $t>2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/example-564.json" "file_sha256" "8754342bf61ad02504e1bb37be04889e9c2fb389a23b0ddd4520bcedf3bd1aee"} {"question" "e-565" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s^2,$ given by the function $a(t) = 6t-3t^2,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s$ when $t=1\\,\\textrm s,$ calculate the maximum velocity $v_{\\text{max}}$ of the particle and determine the time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/example-565.json" "file_sha256" "59c1e076bc2888c584206a61d8e625d5a7fe2f21eff764f133dc0d01d22e6e1b"} {"question" "e-565" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d t} = a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t) = 0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 6t-3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3t(2-t) & =0 \\\\ t & =0,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t> 0$, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $t = 2\\,\\textrm s.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the second derivative test. For this, we find the second derivative of $v(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{″}(t) & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t-3{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =6-6t \\\\ {v}^{″}(2) & =6-6(2) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $v''(2) <      0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=2\\,\\textrm s.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (6t-3{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}-{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1) = 2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(1)^{2}-(1)^{3}+C & =2 \\\\ 3-1+C & =2\\,⟹\\,C=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t) =3t^2-t^3.$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\nv_{\\text{max}}= v(2) = 3(2)^2 - (2)^3 =4\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s\n$$\n\nSo the maximum velocity $v_{\\text{max}}=4\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s$ is reached at $t=2\\,\\textrm s.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/example-565.json" "file_sha256" "59c1e076bc2888c584206a61d8e625d5a7fe2f21eff764f133dc0d01d22e6e1b"} {"question" "e-7226" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s^2,$ given by the function $a(t) =2t-6$, where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=4\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s$ when $t=1\\,\\textrm s$, determine the moments in time when the particle is stationary." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/example-7226.json" "file_sha256" "acc61d9074cd50a65ac65551ed0f17b0ea62bfeb97c52efe39e71e3cb54fdb90"} {"question" "e-7226" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2t-6)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}-6t+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1) =4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =(1)^{2}-6(1)+C \\\\ 4 & =-5+C \\\\ C & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t) = t^2-6t + 9.$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-6t+9 & =0 \\\\ (t-3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ t & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary when $t=3 \\,\\textrm s.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063912/example-7226.json" "file_sha256" "acc61d9074cd50a65ac65551ed0f17b0ea62bfeb97c52efe39e71e3cb54fdb90"}] "usage" {"e-564" [] "e-565" [] "e-7226" []} "basis" 2089 "retractions" [[17592186175704 :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s^2$, given by the function $a(t) = 3t^2+2t-6$, where $t > 0$ is the time in seconds. The positive direction is left to right. If the particle is stationary when $t=0\\,\\textrm s$, determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving to the right."] [17592186175704 :question/worked-solution "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= ∫a(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫(3t^{2} + 2t - 6)dt \\\\\n&= t^{3} + t^{2} - 6t + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the particle is stationary at the time $t=0$. This means $v(0) = 0$, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0 &= (0)^{3} + (0)^{2} - 6(0) + C \\\\\n0 &= C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore the velocity of the particle is\n\n$$\nv(t) = t^3+t^2-6t\n$$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the particle is moving to the *right*, we must solve the inequality $v > 0$. This gives\n\n$$\nt^3+t^2-6t = t(t+3)(t-2) > 0\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t)$, we get the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3582/Source/Images/8f7445db34aa0dcfa29db94a8442208a.png)\n\nThe particle moves to the right when $v(t)$ is positive. So, from the graph, we see that the particle moves to the right when $t>2$."] [17592186175705 :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s^2$, given by the function $a(t) = 6t-3t^2$, where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s$ when $t=1\\,\\textrm s$, calculate the maximum velocity $v_{\\text{max}}$ of the particle and determine the time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity."] [17592186175705 :question/worked-solution "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v$.\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d t} = a(t)\n$$\n\nto find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t) = 0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na(t) &= 0 \\\\\n6t - 3t^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n3t(2 - t) &= 0 \\\\\nt &= 0, 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t> 0$, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when\n\n$$\nt = 2\\,\\textrm s\n$$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the second derivative test. For this, we find the second derivative of $v(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv^{″}(t) &= \\frac{d^{2}v}{dt^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dv}{dt}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(a(t)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(6t - 3t^{2}) \\\\\n&= 6 - 6t \\\\\nv^{″}(2) &= 6 - 6(2) \\\\\n&=-6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $v''(2) < 0$, we conclude that the velocity is maximized when\n\n$$\nt=2\\,\\textrm s\n$$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= ∫a(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫(6t - 3t^{2})dt \\\\\n&= 3t^{2} - t^{3} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1) = 2$. Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3(1)^{2} - (1)^{3} + C &= 2 \\\\\n3 - 1 + C &= 2⟹C = 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nv(t) =3t^2-t^3\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\nv_{\\text{max}}= v(2) = 3(2)^2 - (2)^3 =4\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s\n$$\n\nSo the maximum velocity\n\n$$\nv_{\\text{max}}=4\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s\n$$\n\nis reached at\n\n$$\nt=2\\,\\textrm s\n$$"] [17592186175722 :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s^2$, given by the function $a(t) =2t-6$, where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=4\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s$ when $t=1\\,\\textrm s$, determine the moments in time when the particle is stationary."] [17592186175722 :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when\n\n$$\nv=0\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= ∫a(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫(2t - 6)dt \\\\\n&= t^{2} - 6t + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1) =4$. Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4 &= (1)^{2} - 6(1) + C \\\\\n4 &= -5 + C \\\\\nC &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nv(t) = t^2-6t + 9\n$$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when\n\n$$\nv=0\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^{2} - 6t + 9 &= 0 \\\\\n(t - 3)^{2} &= 0 \\\\\nt &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary when\n\n$$\nt=3 \\,\\textrm s\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
